WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
- [Marten] The ice is
typically around 5 centimeters

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.230
when I skate on it.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:07.064
That sound you hear is actually

00:00:07.064 --> 00:00:08.902
a combination of two sounds.

00:00:08.902 --> 00:00:12.303
It's the sounds from
cracks striking beneath me

00:00:12.303 --> 00:00:16.060
and the vibrations within
the ice plate itself.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.277
It sounds like it's something super sonic.

00:00:18.277 --> 00:00:21.347
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:21.347 --> 00:00:23.312
(skates swooshing)

00:00:23.312 --> 00:00:26.150
- Meet Märten Ajne.
- Hi I'm Märten Ajne.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.410
- [Narrator] He's a mathematician

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.547
who enjoys riskier hobbies than most.

00:00:29.547 --> 00:00:32.817
- [Marten] I been skating
on thin ice for 40 years.

00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:34.850
- [Narrator] When he's skating though,

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:35.924
he's also listening.

00:00:35.924 --> 00:00:38.634
- [Märten] The thinner the
ice, the higher the pitch.

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:41.414
Just about when the ice is to break

00:00:41.414 --> 00:00:44.840
and that will be about 3 centimeters,

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.420
the pitch is at the highest singing.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.310
There's a reportedly highest note

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.234
of the Soprano singing.

00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:52.840
- [Narrator] Märten relies

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.670
on his knowledge as a mathematician

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.490
to calculate the thickness of the ice,

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.605
its flexibility and its temperature.

00:00:58.605 --> 00:01:00.890
- [Märten] Many people tend to freak out

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.150
when they see us skating on thin ice,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.180
but I couldn't be more calm
because I know what I'm doing.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.290
Sometimes the ice is perfectly clear

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:09.800
and I can see fish below,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.897
even a beaver, and also three
divers swimming under the ice.

00:01:13.897 --> 00:01:15.600
- [Narrator] When Märten first started

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.897
there wasn't any physics
or scientific information

00:01:17.897 --> 00:01:20.661
to explain how thin ice
skating is possible.

00:01:20.661 --> 00:01:23.709
He studied the ice for
10 years every winter

00:01:23.709 --> 00:01:25.995
to explain the phenomenon.

00:01:25.995 --> 00:01:30.267
- [Märten] I observed three
things with the thin ice:

00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:33.750
one is that the ice bends,
and the thinner it is

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.010
the more advanced.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.785
The ice needs to be
flexible to support you.

00:01:37.785 --> 00:01:41.120
Second, the first crack
is a true warning signal,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.560
then I know the ice is thin.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.660
And when it gets even
thinner more cracks evoke.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.010
And finally, the ice gives sonorous tone-

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.178
beautiful, eerie tone,
which immediately tells me

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.860
how thick the ice is.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.550
It's called the Coincidence Frequency,

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.363
and it can be calculated mathematically.

00:02:01.198 --> 00:02:03.560
- [Narrator] Märten has skated

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.846
on over 1,800 bodies of
water in North America,

00:02:06.846 --> 00:02:09.459
Norway and Central and Eastern Europe.

00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:11.990
- [Märten] I love skating
for the excitement

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:13.710
and challenges it brings.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.910
It's an opportunity for math
and nature to come together

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.483
and make it understandable for me.

00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:20.660
I've been skating most of my life,

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.290
and I always look forward to next weekend.

